This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   Add BigQuery pipe syntax support to the sql-formatter project at /app. BigQuery pipe syntax chains transformations via `|>` instead of nested clauses; the formatter currently lacks pipe awareness and misformats pipe queries. Specific requirements from the user:
   - Pipe queries start with standalone `FROM`; each subsequent `|>` step occupies its own line at base indentation
   - The pipe operator and clause keyword share the same line
   - The clause body starts on the next line, indented one level deeper, following the same indentation pattern the formatter already uses for that clause type in traditional queries
   - Indented clauses: `WHERE`, `SELECT`, `ORDER BY`, `AGGREGATE`, `EXTEND`, `SET`, `DROP` — body on a new indented line after the keyword
   - One-line clauses: `LIMIT`, `JOIN` and its variants, `AS` — content stays on the same line as the keyword
   - Pipe-exclusive clauses: `AGGREGATE` (with optional nested `GROUP BY` sub-clause requiring its own indentation level), `EXTEND` (computed columns), `SET` (replacing values), `DROP` (removing columns), `AS` (naming intermediates)
   - Pipe queries nest inside parentheses as subqueries; traditional BigQuery formatting remains unchanged
   - `keywordCase` governs all pipe keywords including pipe-exclusive ones
   - `|>` must tokenize as a distinct type, not bitwise `|` plus `>`
   - Pipe clauses produce structured parse nodes; `AGGREGATE` and `EXTEND` are promoted to reserved clauses after `|>`
   - `GROUP BY` within `AGGREGATE` nests as a sub-clause with its own indentation
   - Each `|>` resets to base indentation; semicolons attach after the final pipe step; mixed pipe and traditional statements format independently
   - **IMPORTANT: "Please work on this in a new branch from main and commit everything when you are done."**

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - sql-formatter v15.7.2 architecture: Tokenizer (regex-rule based, rules checked in order) → disambiguateTokens → nearley grammar (grammar.ne → generated grammar.ts, gitignored, regenerated via `yarn grammar`/nearleyc) → AST → ExpressionFormatter with Layout/Indentation
   - Nearley grammar conventions: `_` rule matches optional comments; `clause:*` follows `free_form_sql:*` in `expressions_or_clauses`; terminals return tokens in actions; `unwrap` helper for parenthesized alternatives; `@lexer` with `has(name)` checking `name in TokenType`
   - TokenType enum (token.ts): RESERVED_CLAUSE, RESERVED_SELECT, RESERVED_JOIN, RESERVED_SET_OPERATION, LIMIT, OPERATOR, etc.
   - Indentation model: top-level (clause bodies) vs block-level (parens) indents; `increaseTopLevel`/`decreaseTopLevel`/`increaseBlockLevel`/`decreaseBlockLevel`; Layout WS items (SPACE, NO_SPACE, NEWLINE, MANDATORY_NEWLINE, INDENT, SINGLE_INDENT)
   - Clause format styles: `formatClauseInIndentedStyle` (KW NEWLINE + indented body), `formatClauseInOnelineStyle` (KW SPACE + body same line), `formatClauseInTabularStyle` (oneline + top-level increase); `formatLimitClause` (dedicated; count on next line in standard style, same line in tabular); `formatSetOperation` (KW at base, children at base); `formatJoin` (newline + KW + SPACE, body same line)
   - Dialect config: `DialectFormatOptions` → `ProcessedDialectFormatOptions` (Record<string,boolean> lookups) via processDialectFormatOptions in dialect.ts
   - BigQuery specifics: `operators` list, `postProcess` hook (existing: combineParameterizedTypes, detectArraySubscripts), `expandPhrases` for keyword phrase expansion, reservedJoins = ['JOIN','LEFT JOIN','LEFT OUTER JOIN','RIGHT JOIN','RIGHT OUTER JOIN','FULL JOIN','FULL OUTER JOIN','INNER JOIN','CROSS JOIN']
   - Ambiguity avoidance: PIPE token must NOT be in free_form_sql so clause-body/pipe-step boundaries are unambiguous
   - InlineLayout throws InlineLayoutError on any WS.NEWLINE → parens containing clauses always format multiline
   - Test infra: jest + ts-jest; `yarn test` = `yarn grammar && jest`; feature tests in test/features/

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - `/app/src/lexer/token.ts` (EDITED)
      - Added PIPE token type. Edit made:
      ```typescript
      OPERATOR = 'OPERATOR',
      PIPE = 'PIPE', // The pipe operator |> used in pipe queries
      COMMA = 'COMMA',
      ```
   - `/app/src/languages/bigquery/bigquery.formatter.ts` (EDITED)
      - Core dialect file. `reservedJoins` defined as `expandPhrases(['JOIN', '{LEFT | RIGHT | FULL} [OUTER] JOIN', '{INNER | CROSS} JOIN'])`. Changes made:
      ```typescript
      operators: ['&', '|', '^', '~', '>>', '<<', '||', '=>', '|>'],
      postProcess,
        },
        formatOptions: {
          onelineClauses: [...standardOnelineClauses, ...tabularOnelineClauses],
          tabularOnelineClauses,
          pipeOnelineClauses: ['AS', ...reservedJoins],
        },
      };

      function postProcess(tokens: Token[]): Token[] {
        return detectArraySubscripts(combineParameterizedTypes(promotePipeClauses(tokens)));
      }

      // Converts the pipe operator (|>) into a PIPE token,
      // and promotes AGGREGATE, EXTEND and AS to clause keywords
      // when they immediately follow a pipe operator.
      // See: https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/pipe-operators
      function promotePipeClauses(tokens: Token[]): Token[] {
        const result: Token[] = [];
        let prevToken = EOF_TOKEN;
        for (const token of tokens) {
          let converted = token;
          if (converted.type === TokenType.OPERATOR && converted.text === '|>') {
            converted = { ...converted, type: TokenType.PIPE };
          } else if (prevToken.type === TokenType.PIPE && isPipeClauseKeyword(converted)) {
            converted = {
              ...converted,
              type: TokenType.RESERVED_CLAUSE,
              text: converted.text.toUpperCase(),
            };
          }
          if (!isComment(converted)) {
            prevToken = converted;
          }
          result.push(converted);
        }
        return result;
      }

      // AGGREGATE and EXTEND are regular identifiers outside of pipe queries,
      // while AS is a regular keyword. After a pipe operator,
      // they all become clause keywords.
      function isPipeClauseKeyword(token: Token): boolean {
        if (token.type === TokenType.RESERVED_KEYWORD) {
          return token.text === 'AS';
        } else if (token.type === TokenType.IDENTIFIER) {
          return token.text.toUpperCase() === 'AGGREGATE' || token.text.toUpperCase() === 'EXTEND';
        } else {
          return false;
        }
      }

      const isComment = (token: Token): boolean =>
        token.type === TokenType.LINE_COMMENT ||
        token.type === TokenType.BLOCK_COMMENT ||
        token.type === TokenType.DISABLE_COMMENT;
      ```
      - Note: EOF_TOKEN is already imported in this file (used by detectArraySubscripts).
   - `/app/src/parser/ast.ts` (EDITED)
      - Added `pipe_clause = 'pipe_clause',` to NodeType enum (after `clause`); added interface after ClauseNode:
      ```typescript
      // A single step of a pipe query, like:
      //
      //   |> WHERE x = 1
      //
      // or, with a nested sub-clause (GROUP BY inside AGGREGATE):
      //
      //   |> AGGREGATE SUM(y) GROUP BY z
      export interface PipeClauseNode extends BaseNode {
        type: NodeType.pipe_clause;
        clause: ClauseNode | LimitClauseNode | SetOperationNode;
        subClause?: ClauseNode;
      }
      ```
      - Added `| PipeClauseNode` to the AstNode union (after ClauseNode). Note: LimitClauseNode has field `limitKw` (not nameKw); ClauseNode/SetOperationNode have `nameKw` + `children`.
   - `/app/src/parser/grammar.ne` (EDITED)
      - Modified `clause` rule and added pipe rules:
      ```
      clause ->
        ( limit_clause
        | select_clause
        | other_clause
        | set_operation
        | pipe_clause ) {% unwrap %}

      # A step of a pipe query, like:
      #
      #   |> WHERE x = 1
      #
      # The clause keyword is formatted on the same line as the pipe operator.
      pipe_clause -> %PIPE clause {%
        ([, clauseNode]) => ({
          type: NodeType.pipe_clause,
          clause: clauseNode,
        })
      %}

      # JOIN (and its variants) can't be matched by the clause rule above,
      # because in regular SQL it's part of the FROM clause body.
      # In pipe queries it starts a pipe step on its own, like:
      #
      #   |> JOIN other ON t.id = other.id
      pipe_clause -> %PIPE %RESERVED_JOIN free_form_sql:* {%
        ([, joinToken, children]) => ({
          type: NodeType.pipe_clause,
          clause: {
            type: NodeType.clause,
            nameKw: toKeywordNode(joinToken),
            children,
          },
        })
      %}
      ```
   - `/app/src/parser/grammar.ts` (REGENERATED via `yarn grammar`; gitignored)
      - Verified contains `declare var PIPE: any;` and both pipe_clause rules.
   - `/app/src/parser/createParser.ts` (EDITED)
      - Imports now: `import { AstNode, ClauseNode, NodeType, PipeClauseNode, StatementNode } from './ast.js';` and `import { last } from '../utils.js';`
      - `parse` now returns `reparentPipeGroupBys(results[0])` on single result
      - Added:
      ```typescript
      // In pipe queries, a GROUP BY that follows an AGGREGATE step
      // is part of that step, like:
      //
      //   FROM t |> AGGREGATE SUM(x) GROUP BY y
      //
      // The grammar produces the GROUP BY as a sibling clause,
      // so we reparent it under the AGGREGATE pipe clause.
      function reparentPipeGroupBys(statements: StatementNode[]): StatementNode[] {
        return statements.map(statement => {
          const children: AstNode[] = [];
          for (const child of statement.children) {
